#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119526 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fcf3704-4856-5095-a6f4-7ec20d81f3c4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing an Unknown in Terms of 2x2 Determinants"}] :question/id #uuid "4efedc4e-19e9-552c-bc27-814df953e141" :question/math-academy-id "e-4575" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations\n${5x + 2y =-1; - 3x + 3y = 9$.\nExpress $y$ in terms of $2 \\times 2$ determinants." :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n{5x + 2y =-1; - 3x + 3y = 9\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct $D_y$, we replace the second column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{y} &= \\begin{vmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, according to Cramer's rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{D_{y}}{D} = \\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119528 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9b0f65-d8a3-57c4-88c5-2738b0938c0d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 2x2 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "311ba12f-de22-580c-9e0d-6766f7864d1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4578" :question/problem "Let $\\langle x,3 \\rangle$ be the solution of the system of equations below.\n${5x - 2y = 4; x + ky = 8$\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k$?\n\n1. $\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{vmatrix}$\n2. $\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{vmatrix}$\n3. $\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{vmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= \\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{D_y}{D}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{y} &= \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 8 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{D_{y}}{D} \\\\\n3 &= \\frac{36}{\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{vmatrix} &= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119585 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7580024-f357-57d4-94f2-85c25e2c144b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Parts of Cramer's Rule Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "d81e32a1-bd93-5a95-bb4a-e8df9063b5aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-5230" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n${x - y = 2; 2x + y = 1$.\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as\n$\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}$\nExpress $D_x$ in terms of a $2 \\times 2$ determinant." :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n{x - y = 2; 2x + y = 1\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct $D_x$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{x} &= \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fcf3704-4856-5095-a6f4-7ec20d81f3c4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing an Unknown in Terms of 2x2 Determinants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3bcd641-63a1-5177-9aa8-64f94b97d284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 15 \\\\ 2 & -9\\e